Dear Make: Editors,

Solidon3D 0.3.0 has been released. It is a local desktop application for
adapting downloaded 3D-printing models and constructing printable parts from
dimensions, sketches and tested components. Rather than send a general product
announcement, I would like to pitch a reproducible workshop article: print one
small gauge, measure the clearances that actually work on your printer and
filament, then reuse those values in every later hole, pin, bearing seat and
snap fit.

Solidon3D generates stepped fit, wall-thickness and overhang calibration
pieces. The maker records the useful steps in a material profile. From then
on, a downloaded STL can have an existing hole recognised and resized, a
heat-set insert can use the measured press fit, and a print-in-place hinge can
inherit the tested clearance. Every change remains a reversible operation, so
the result can be adjusted after the first print rather than rebuilt.

The exercise can stand on its own, but it also shows what changed since
Solidon’s first 0.1 demo. The 0.3.0 release brings the selected user-visible
change count to 220: in-viewport sketching, reusable parametric parts made
from history without code, matching printable screws and nuts, per-spool
process data in 3MF, and Windows, macOS and Linux builds.

Solidon works without an account or cloud storage. Its optional AI chat is not
required for this project; geometry is always calculated by deterministic
code.

Overview: https://solidon3d.de/en/

I can deliver a complete article test kit: calibration and comparison models,
